WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.520
And when thou would solace gather-
When our child's first accents flow-&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.440
Wilt thou teach her to say father?
Though his care she must forgo.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.880
Those are the words of a poet named Lord&nbsp;
Byron written about his daughter Ada as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.800
was separating from her mother&nbsp;
just weeks after Ada was born.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.120
Ada would marry and go on to become Ada King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.600
Countess of Lovelace or known as Ada&nbsp;
Lovelace and she's often referred to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.240
as the first ever computer programmer.
That is all I had heard about her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.840
until recently when I wondered, what does&nbsp;
that really mean? and is it even true?

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.440
Well the best way to find out is to&nbsp;
read the document that she's famous for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.560
It's the only paper that she published&nbsp;
and it's called Sketch of the Analytical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:01.840
Engine. It is this document that will allow us to&nbsp;
understand Ada's role in the history of computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.360
so in this video I would like&nbsp;
to go through it with you.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.360
It describes the analytical engine invented&nbsp;
by Charles Babbage which was a monstrously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:18.000
ambitious steam-powered computer. I will&nbsp;
cover the mechanical aspects of the engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.200
just enough to be able to understand some&nbsp;
of the programs that Ada wrote for it.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:30.880
Gossip about her private affairs, gambling, and&nbsp;
interesting philosophies, although fascinating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.040
will have to be left for another video.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.440
A Sketch of the Analytical Engine was&nbsp;
published in 1843 and the first part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.640
it was a paper published by an Italian&nbsp;
mathematician who would actually go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:52.720
to become the prime minister of Italy, Luigi&nbsp;
Menabrea. Ada translated his work into english&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.640
and then added her own notes to it and these&nbsp;
notes were twice as long as the original paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.880
At the time ada had already been acquainted&nbsp;
with Charles Babbage for a number of years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.720
and had been working alongside&nbsp;
him to understand the engine.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.360
It was in these notes that she included an&nbsp;
algorithm that could use the engine to compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.880
Bernoulli numbers and it is this which is called&nbsp;
the first computer program. She also notes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.800
the engine could be used to do things&nbsp;
other than arithmetic and computations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.880
She has this idea that quantities other than&nbsp;
numbers could be represented in the engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:42.240
and could therefore go on to be used for things&nbsp;
like composing music. This insight and vision a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:49.120
hundred years before Alan Turing would come along&nbsp;
with his own ideas of the modern computer are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:55.200
really quite remarkable and is something that she&nbsp;
definitely deserves to be credited for as well.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.880
So let's start at the beginning. It was Charles&nbsp;
Babbage who was the mastermind behind this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:07.200
first computer and he was frustrated by&nbsp;
mistakes that crept into logarithm tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:14.640
used back then for calculations. He wanted to&nbsp;
"execute by means of machinery the mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.520
branch of scientific labors, reserving for pure&nbsp;
intellect that which depends on reasoning".&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.680
So really he wants to free&nbsp;
up the scientist's mind.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.840
Charles Babbage first came&nbsp;
up with the difference engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:38.480
and in this example here we have the series of&nbsp;
whole square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:45.760
If we find the difference between each of these&nbsp;
numbers we'll get a new series 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:52.480
the difference between each of these numbers is&nbsp;
always equal to 2. It is shown in this table here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.680
and from this if we were given just the&nbsp;
numbers 2, 5, and 9 we would be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.760
reconstruct this entire series of square&nbsp;
numbers just through a series of additions.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:15.360
For the machine we would have three dials A, B&nbsp;
and C and also two hammers. So if we start by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:25.280
placing needle C on 2, needle B on 5 and needle&nbsp;
A on 9, then the hammer on C will strike twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:33.280
adding 2 onto needle B. So B will now read&nbsp;
7, it will then strike its hammer 7 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:40.640
adding 7 onto 9 and giving us the result&nbsp;
16. If we recommence these operations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.880
beginning with the needle C which is always to&nbsp;
be left on 2 we will successively reproduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.360
series of whole square numbers by&nbsp;
means of a very simple mechanism.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.280
Babbage himself ran out of money to ever finish&nbsp;
building the difference engine although he did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.360
have a small model of it. Since then some&nbsp;
clever people have finished it for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:09.360
and made a complete version. Babbage kind of&nbsp;
got distracted by his idea for a second type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:16.320
engine, one that could do a lot more and was much&nbsp;
more ambitious, this was the analytical engine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.600
The analytical engine can perform addition,&nbsp;
subtraction, multiplication and division&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.720
and "is therefore capable of performing&nbsp;
every species of numerical calculation".

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.480
For the analytical engine we have a series&nbsp;
of vertical columns consisting of a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:40.480
circular disks. The first disk represents units,&nbsp;
the second tens, the third hundreds and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.640
Babbage's notes showed as many as 40 disks&nbsp;
so you could have quite some precision. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.320
haven't had the idea of using binary&nbsp;
numbers so everything is in decimal.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:01.520
We denote these columns with V for variable and&nbsp;
for example if we wanted to add the numbers on V1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:08.240
and V4 together we would then place the result&nbsp;
on a third column we could call that V7.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.880
The act of adding the numbers together will&nbsp;
occur at a place on the engine called the mill.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.200
"The mill is the portion&nbsp;
of the machine which works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.760
and the columns of variables constitute that&nbsp;
where the results are represented and arranged".

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:34.000
So in modern computing notation the mill may&nbsp;
be called the CPU and the columns of variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.240
this big extension out the back of the machine,&nbsp;
would be called the store or the memory.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.720
You may wonder how the machine knows which&nbsp;
operations to perform and which variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:53.360
to include and the solution has been taken from&nbsp;
Jacquard's apparatus. What they're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:59.840
here is using punch cards that were first used in&nbsp;
Jacquard's loom, something that was used to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:07.040
intricate patterns in lace and other materials&nbsp;
by using a series of cards as the instructions.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:13.600
There are two main types of cards that will&nbsp;
allow us to program the engine, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.280
are the operation cards and these are telling&nbsp;
the machine if you want to perform addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:26.640
subtraction, multiplication or division and the&nbsp;
second type of card is the card of the variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.520
which indicate to the machine the columns&nbsp;
on which the results are to be represented.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.320
There's then a fairly brief example and you may&nbsp;
even want to call this the first computer program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:44.160
since it's the first example in the notes but&nbsp;
it's much more simple than the Bernoulli program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:51.120
which will appear later. In this we have two&nbsp;
simultaneous equations and say that we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.840
to solve for x. Well we can rearrange these&nbsp;
equations into this fraction here, then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:06.400
assign our eight variables to eight different&nbsp;
columns. n and n' have been represented twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.760
probably because they will need to be used&nbsp;
twice in separate parts of the calculation.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.520
This table then gives an idea of the cards that&nbsp;
would be needed to execute this. Here we have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:23.440
number of the operation so first step, second&nbsp;
step, third step and so on then in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:28.880
column here these are our set of operation&nbsp;
cards. They're just saying that we're either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:35.840
performing a multiplication, a subtraction or a&nbsp;
division. And these are the cards of the variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.360
telling the machine which variables are&nbsp;
involved in each of these operations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:49.840
so we have here V2 times V4 and that&nbsp;
result is going to be put on the column V8.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.040
This box here just shows what we're doing.&nbsp;
That was the step of multiplying d by n'.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.600
Following all these steps and the last one&nbsp;
will end up giving you the result for x.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.600
Since the cards just tell the machine what columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:11.520
and what operations to do we still need to&nbsp;
enter the initial numerical data ourselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.760
so there's still a little bit of&nbsp;
room for a human error in here.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.920
What's interesting is that the method of running&nbsp;
the engine is actually Turing complete which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.520
wasn't a phrase that existed at the time but&nbsp;
essentially means it would be able to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.640
any computation although in this case&nbsp;
it is limited by being steam powered.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:41.120
We are now on to the first of Ada's notes,&nbsp;
this one is note A. It is quite philosophical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:48.800
and poetic. She says in it that "the machine&nbsp;
might act upon other things besides number...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:56.080
Supposing for instance that the fundamental&nbsp;
relations of pitched sounds in the science of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:04.080
harmony and musical composition were susceptible&nbsp;
of such expression and adaptations the engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:11.440
might compose elaborate and scientific pieces&nbsp;
of music of any degree of complexity or extent".

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:18.240
She says that "whether the inventor of this engine&nbsp;
had any such views in his mind while working out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.520
the invention or whether he may subsequently&nbsp;
ever have regarded it under this phase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:30.400
we do not know but it is one that forcibly&nbsp;
occurred to ourselves on becoming acquainted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:36.400
the means through which analytical combinations&nbsp;
are actually attained by the mechanism".

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.360
She's saying that there are some&nbsp;
things that are very clear to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.240
but she's not sure if Charles&nbsp;
Babbage even shares the same views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.600
She even speaks of the intrinsic&nbsp;
beauty of the mathematics behind this.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:58.080
She says that "this science constitutes&nbsp;
the language through which alone we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:02.480
adequately express the great&nbsp;
facts of the natural world...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:08.960
Those who thus think on mathematical truth as the&nbsp;
instrument through which the weak mind of a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:16.080
can most effectually read his Creator's works&nbsp;
will regard with special interest all that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:22.640
tend to facilitate the translation of its&nbsp;
principles into explicit practical forms".

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:29.040
I love this sentence here where she says that&nbsp;
"the analytical engine weaves algebraic patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:36.160
just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and&nbsp;
leaves". She's seeing how they can take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:42.640
beautiful patterns from lace and material and&nbsp;
translate that into the beauty of mathematics.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:48.560
The other big idea that she deserves to&nbsp;
be remembered for is recognizing that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:55.920
analytical engine is unlike anything before it.&nbsp;
she says here that "the engine does not occupy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:02.320
common ground with mere calculating machines"&nbsp;
and she spends a lot of the notes pointing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:08.400
the differences between the difference engine&nbsp;
and this much more powerful analytical engine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:15.600
Doing so was important in its own right but it&nbsp;
was also slightly political in a way because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:21.440
Charles Babbage was trying really hard to get&nbsp;
enough funding to build his analytical engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.680
and with his track record of running&nbsp;
out of money to build the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:32.400
engine he was really struggling to convince&nbsp;
people of its value and of its difference.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:38.800
Ada then says something which still rings&nbsp;
true today in the pursuit of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:45.520
convince people that doing research or building&nbsp;
machines or projects that might not have as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:52.000
immediate value can still bring a lot of value&nbsp;
down the line that we are not even aware of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:59.280
She says "those who incline to very strictly&nbsp;
utilitarian views may perhaps feel that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:05.840
peculiar powers of the analytical engine bear&nbsp;
upon questions of abstract and speculative science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:14.000
rather than upon those involving everyday and&nbsp;
ordinary human interests. These persons being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:21.040
likely to possess but little sympathy, or possibly&nbsp;
acquaintance, with any branches of science which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:27.680
they do not find to be useful (according to&nbsp;
their definition of that word) may conceive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:34.400
that the undertaking of that engine now that the&nbsp;
other one is already in progress would be a barren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:41.440
and unproductive laying out of yet more money and&nbsp;
labor" however she continues that while some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:48.720
the results of the analytical engine may be known&nbsp;
now "others which it may not yet be possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:54.640
foresee but which would be brought forth by&nbsp;
the daily increasing requirements of science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:00.880
and with practical acquaintance with the powers&nbsp;
of the engine were it in actual existence".

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:09.040
She sounds like anyone today trying to justify&nbsp;
some of these more abstract or blue sky research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:16.320
ideas in mathematics or even things like&nbsp;
investing in space technology and exploration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:23.760
Unfortunately due to the lack of funds and lack&nbsp;
of mechanical and technical abilities back then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.960
they weren't able to actually build the&nbsp;
analytical engine and I wonder what kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:35.040
of insights and developments might have happened&nbsp;
if they actually had one of these in existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:40.800
It would be quite a long time before&nbsp;
we would converge back on these ideas.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:48.000
Let's move on to Ada's last note the famous&nbsp;
note G. It starts off with a comment that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:54.720
surprisingly relevant here she says that&nbsp;
"in considering any new subject there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.720 --> 00:15:01.920
frequently a tendency first to overrate what&nbsp;
we find to be already interesting or remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:09.440
and secondly by a sort of natural reaction&nbsp;
to undervalue the true state of the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:14.800
when we do discover that our notions have&nbsp;
surpassed those that were really tenable".

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:20.320
She may be talking about the engine and&nbsp;
not wanting to overstate its abilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:28.400
but the sentiment also is true for her role in the&nbsp;
history of computing. With the claims that she is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:36.400
the first ever computer programmer perhaps she has&nbsp;
been sometimes overrated but then when people look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:41.920
more into the case and start to say well you&nbsp;
know Charles Babbage has written programs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:48.880
his own in some of his notes there is a risk&nbsp;
to undervalue the true work that Ada has done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:54.080
and we shouldn't do that because through&nbsp;
these notes I hope that it comes across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.080 --> 00:16:00.880
that she speaks with intelligence, with a lot&nbsp;
of knowledge of the analytical engine and of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:08.080
potential in a way that nobody else at the time&nbsp;
did and she definitely deserves to be valued.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:15.680
And now finally we get to the really juicy&nbsp;
stuff which is this computer program to compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:21.840
the numbers of Bernoulli. The Bernoulli numbers&nbsp;
are involved with trying to calculate the sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:27.520
of positive integers to any given power.&nbsp;
Throughout history a lot of people tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:34.240
to do this and it was really difficult. Jacob&nbsp;
Bernoulli came up with a way to do this which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:38.960
wasn't quite easy but at least&nbsp;
was easier than any other method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:45.600
He found that the sum of the first positive n&nbsp;
integers to any given power followed a pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:53.120
and the coefficients of each term in this pattern&nbsp;
could be broken down into two factors. One set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:59.840
of these factors the As could be obtained&nbsp;
from Pascal's triangle and the other set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:05.760
the Bs were called the Bernoulli numbers and&nbsp;
they themselves followed a pattern and could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:12.240
be worked out using what's in the box up here. Ada&nbsp;
herself acknowledges in the notes that this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:18.880
rather complicated example to be giving and not&nbsp;
that easy to follow the mathematical derivation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:24.800
but she gives it as a way to illustrate&nbsp;
the very powerful abilities of the engine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:32.160
Her program computes these Bernoulli numbers and&nbsp;
it does so in terms of all the preceding ones.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:37.600
She says that she "appends to this note a&nbsp;
diagram and table containing the details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:46.000
of the computation for B7 given B1,&nbsp;
B3 and B5, and this is that program.

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:54.800
It's computing B to the 2n minus 1 in the case&nbsp;
of n being equal to 4. We have the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.800 --> 00:18:02.240
operation that we're up to and we have the&nbsp;
operation cards in here, multiply, subtract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:09.520
add and divide. We then have the variable cards&nbsp;
telling us which variables are involved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:17.760
operation and where their result will go. We also&nbsp;
have this indication of change in the value on any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:25.600
variable to keep track of variable columns that&nbsp;
are changing. We have our statement of results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:33.200
to see what step in the computation we are up to&nbsp;
and then we have written here what our variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:42.720
actually are. In our case there will be six inputs&nbsp;
into the program. We will need to put in 1, 2 our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:52.720
n which is 4 and B1, B3 and B5. That's what we&nbsp;
need to get to B7 although we are actually working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:59.600
out these values as we go, if we have stopped&nbsp;
earlier in the process before we got to B7.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:09.280
In this very first step where we multiply V2&nbsp;
by V3 we multiply 2 by n and we put the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:17.040
2n onto these three columns here because we're&nbsp;
going to want to use those later. We return the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:22.320
two and the n back to where they came from because&nbsp;
we're also going to want to use those again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:29.920
In the case where we don't use the value again&nbsp;
we replace it with a zero. So we proceed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:38.240
some of the steps of the algebra here and the last&nbsp;
line in this first section here is doing n minus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:45.360
one equal to three. We're taking our column where&nbsp;
n is stored so it was initially four and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:53.200
subtracting one from it to be left with a new n&nbsp;
which is equal to three. Because n is now equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.200 --> 00:20:01.920
3 we're going to continue on the process and then&nbsp;
in the next step we'll decrease n to 2 and so on.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:11.680
The section of lines down here 13 to 23 are very&nbsp;
interesting because every time n increases we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:18.960
need to do an additional repetition of these&nbsp;
operations 13 to 23. Because of that when n is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:27.840
bigger than 2, 25 operation cards are used but no&nbsp;
more are needed regardless of how great n may be.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:33.760
We will however have to add more variable&nbsp;
cards every time n goes up and Ada refers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:40.880
to this as being a cycle of operations and since&nbsp;
these operations use the same pairs of columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:45.280
they are actually a cycle of variable cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:51.920
so this computer kind of has loops. It might&nbsp;
not be a for loop or a while loop but it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:58.960
a loop of cards that can be fed back into&nbsp;
the machine to continue computing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:04.880
cycle of operations over and over again and&nbsp;
down here she shows what that looks like.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:08.320
She says that "applying the notation for cycles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:14.080
we may express the operations for computing the&nbsp;
numbers of the newly in the following manner".

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:22.880
For B1, B3, B5 and B7 we all have the same initial&nbsp;
set of seven operations, we finish with the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:28.640
pair of operations at the end, in the middle we&nbsp;
might have this set of operations from 8 to 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:36.720
and then a cycle of operations repeating those&nbsp;
from line 13 to 23 with a general formula down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:44.080
here to compute any Bernoulli number. And what&nbsp;
you're looking at here is the seed of computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:50.640
programming that would go on to be so prevalent&nbsp;
and so important to run our modern world.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:55.760
Ada died nine years after the paper was&nbsp;
published but nobody involved in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:01.040
project lived long enough to see the dream&nbsp;
be realized in fact we still haven't built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:06.320
a version of the analytical engine although&nbsp;
I think it is still a dream of many people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:13.840
Ada was motivated and highly intelligent and&nbsp;
she was definitely the first person to clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:22.400
communicate the ideas of a substantial computer&nbsp;
program. Charles Babbage had of course written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:29.040
smaller programs in his own notes since he is the&nbsp;
person who came up with how the engine would work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:35.200
and he did help Ada with a lot of the&nbsp;
mathematics but it was her who wrote it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:42.240
and wrote it down in a way that made it clear what&nbsp;
this engine was capable of and maybe it was her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:48.480
who saw more than Babbage did what really the&nbsp;
potential was that they were dealing with.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:55.920
It was very rare in these times for a woman to&nbsp;
even have a mathematical education but Ada did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:02.800
and the reason that she did was because Ada's&nbsp;
mother tried very hard to steer Ada away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:10.160
from becoming like her poet father Lord Byron.&nbsp;
She didn't want ada to study poetry or literature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:15.760
or things like that, instead she steered&nbsp;
her into studying mathematics and science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:23.120
hoping that that wouldn't result in her sharing&nbsp;
the same madness that ada's mother saw in ada's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:30.880
father. However it wasn't so easy to completely&nbsp;
remove poetic tendencies from Ada because she saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:39.360
her approach kind of as a poetic science. Perhaps&nbsp;
it was those poetic tendencies that allowed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:46.080
to see the far-reaching potentials of&nbsp;
this technology and to see beyond its pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:52.880
mathematical abilities. In the end Ada chose to&nbsp;
be buried alongside her father and although their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:57.920
accomplishments were very different, I guess&nbsp;
there was a thread that tied them together.

